This paper investigates the existence of nontrivial solutions to a modified Choquard type problem that incorporates higher-order derivatives. Specifically, we consider the equation \begin{align*}
\begin{cases}
-\Delta^2 u = \lambda(1+\epsilon f(x))^{\beta p-1}(1+\epsilon f(x))^{ \beta q-1} u \quad \text{in} \ \ \Omega,\\
u = 0 \quad \text{on} \ \ \partial \Omega,
\end{cases}
\end{align*} under certain symmetry conditions on the domain and a nonlinearity term defined in $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N$, where $N \geq 4$. Here, $0 < \epsilon \ll 1$ and the parameters $\beta, p, q$ satisfy specific relationships with $N, \beta p < N$, and $q < p < p^*=\frac{N+2}{N-2}$. We leverage variational methods and a compactness result to establish the existence of multiple solutions in a specific functional space.
